                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_439\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit changes the loop condition in NativeND2Reader.java's metadata-parsing\ncode from:\n\n    while (sb.charAt(sb.length() - 1) != ':' && sb.indexOf(\"_\") < 0 && i < lines.length) {\n\nto:\n\n    while ((sb.length() == 0 || sb.charAt(sb.length() - 1) != ':') && sb.indexOf(\"_\") < 0 && i < lines.length) {\n\nThis guards against calling `charAt(sb.length() - 1)` on an empty StringBuffer, which\nthrows a StringIndexOutOfBoundsException.\n\ncandidate_439 is the commit that introduced this exact vulnerable line. Prior to this\ncommit, the same logic used a plain String `key` and the loop condition was\n`!key.endsWith(\":\")`, which is safe even when `key` is an empty string (it simply\nreturns false rather than throwing). candidate_439 refactored this code (and similar\nstring-concatenation patterns across several other readers) to use StringBuffer for\nperformance, and in doing so replaced the safe `.endsWith(\":\")` check with\n`sb.charAt(sb.length() - 1) != ':'`, which is unsafe when `sb` can be empty (e.g. when\n`lines[i]` is an empty token from the `textString.split(\" \")` call, producing runs of\nempty strings that get appended into an otherwise-empty StringBuffer).\n\ncandidate_440 in the candidate set is actually identical to the fix commit itself\n(the one-line guard fix), confirming that the bug was live in the code from\ncandidate_439 onward until it was patched. Thus candidate_439 is the first commit\nthat introduced the problematic code pattern.\n"
